Fix IndexError in is_nfs_path when `df` wraps a long device name.

DeepSpeed runs `df -T <path>` and reads the filesystem type from the second
column of the second line. BusyBox `df` wraps a device name longer than ~20
characters onto its own line, so that line holds only the device, `.split()`
returns a single element, and `[1]` raises IndexError.

This kills the process at `import deepspeed`, because the call sits at module
scope in matmul_ext.py -- and it only ever exists to print an NFS warning.

Long device names are normal on AWS: the EBS device name is derived from the
volume id, e.g. vol-05d356c8837b6754f -> /dev/dvol05d356c8837b6754f (25 chars).

`df -P` is POSIX and guarantees one line per filesystem, suppressing the wrap.
BusyBox honours both -P and -T. Verified on an affected node:

    df -T  /mount  -> second line has 1 field  (device wrapped)
    df -PT /mount  -> second line has 7 fields (single line)

Upstream issue: https://github.com/deepspeedai/DeepSpeed/issues/8249

--- a/deepspeed/ops/transformer/inference/triton/matmul_ext.py
+++ b/deepspeed/ops/transformer/inference/triton/matmul_ext.py
@@ -35,7 +35,7 @@
 
     # Use the 'df' command to find the file system type for the given path
     try:
-        output = subprocess.check_output(['df', '-T', path], encoding='utf-8', stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)
+        output = subprocess.check_output(['df', '-PT', path], encoding='utf-8', stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)
     except (subprocess.CalledProcessError, FileNotFoundError):
         return False  # Command failed or 'df' not available
 
